---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="Responsible use · FloodWatch.PH" pathname="/safety" description="How to use FloodWatch.PH flood data responsibly: how to cite it, what language is fair, and what the data cannot support.">
  <article class="max-w-prose mx-auto px-6 pt-8 pb-12">
    <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-ink-500 mb-3 font-mono">responsible use &middot; v1 &middot; 2026</p>
    <h1 class="text-3xl mb-3 text-ink-900">Using flood data responsibly.</h1>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
      A satellite flood map is useful, and it has limits. This page explains what FloodWatch.PH can and cannot support, what language is fair when citing or reporting from this data, and how LGU planners and journalists should frame findings.
    </p>

    <p class="text-[11px] text-ink-500 mb-8 leading-relaxed italic border-l-2 border-ink-300/40 pl-4">
      Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data. Patterns may have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-it-supports" class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">What the data supports</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-6">
      <li><strong>Transparency and research.</strong> Comparing what the model predicts against what past records show, to find communities predicted to flood, but barely on record. Informing LGU disaster preparedness planning. Supporting academic research on flood risk in the Philippines. (The official-hazard-map comparison is a later step.)</li>
      <li><strong>Spotting patterns by province.</strong> Which provinces show recurring flooding in the satellite record. Where the prediction-vs-record gap is widest. Who and what is exposed (people and built-up land) and that gap are summed up by province; a city and barangay breakdown is a later refinement.</li>
      <li><strong>A starting point for deeper checking.</strong> Flagging provinces that need local verification: field surveys, local disaster-risk records, requests for official hazard studies.</li>
      <li><strong>Journalism with the caveats stated.</strong> Reporting on the gap between what the model predicts and what past records show, with the methodology caveats clearly stated. See the citing section below.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="what-it-cannot-support" class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">What the data cannot support</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-6">
      <li><strong>Engineering or insurance decisions.</strong> This is not an engineering flood risk study. Flood depth is not in the data. Infrastructure weak points are not modeled. Do not use FloodWatch.PH as a substitute for a formal hydrological study.</li>
      <li><strong>Accusations of government negligence.</strong> The prediction-vs-record gap measures a difference between a model and the past record. It is not proof of wrongdoing. A thin past record may mean past floods went unrecorded, not that the area is safe, and this version does not compare against the official hazard maps at all (that comparison is a later step).</li>
      <li><strong>Claims about specific households or people.</strong> All data is summed up by province. No household or home can be identified, and none is published.</li>
      <li><strong>Forecasts or early warning.</strong> This is past and event-specific data. It is not a forecast system.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="language" class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">Appropriate language</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">Use conservative, verifiable language:</p>
    <div class="bg-paper-alt border border-ink-300/40 p-4 mb-4 text-sm">
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <div>
          <p class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-500 font-mono mb-2">appropriate</p>
          <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-ink-700">
            <li>"Flooding seen by Sentinel-1 radar (it sees through typhoon clouds)"</li>
            <li>"Open water seen by satellite during Typhoon Carina"</li>
            <li>"Warrants independent verification"</li>
            <li>"Predicted to flood, but barely on record"</li>
            <li>"Province-level estimate of who and what is exposed"</li>
            <li>"The data suggests repeated flooding; local verification is recommended"</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-500 font-mono mb-2">inappropriate</p>
          <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-ink-700">
            <li>"Confirmed flooded" (without field verification)</li>
            <li>"The government hid the hazard map"</li>
            <li>"Definitely at risk of flooding"</li>
            <li>"[Specific household] was flooded"</li>
            <li>"Proven negligence"</li>
            <li>"Official data is wrong"</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="citing" class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">How to cite</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      For research and journalism:
    </p>
    <pre class="bg-paper-alt border border-ink-300/40 p-4 text-xs font-mono overflow-x-auto mb-3">FloodWatch.PH (2026). Observed flood extent: Super Typhoon Carina (Gaemi),
July-August 2024, Metro Manila + Bulacan + Pampanga. Derived from
Copernicus Sentinel-1 radar, rivers and lakes removed. CC-BY-4.0.
https://github.com/xmpuspus/floodwatch-ph</pre>
    <p class="text-sm text-ink-500 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Include the disclaimer: "Observed flood extent derived from public satellite data. Patterns may have legitimate explanations; figures warrant independent verification."
    </p>

    <h2 id="lgu" class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">For LGU disaster planners</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      FloodWatch.PH is most useful as a starting point for local verification, not a final answer. Suggested workflow:
    </p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
      <li>Find provinces in the prediction-vs-record gap that are predicted to flood, but barely on record (the model predicts flooding, the past record is thin).</li>
      <li>Cross-reference with local disaster-response records: which areas received evacuation orders or emergency funds during Carina 2024?</li>
      <li>Request the official hazard study for flagged provinces from MGB / UP NOAH / PAGASA if not already on file (this is the comparison FloodWatch.PH plans to add later).</li>
      <li>Commission a local field check for the highest-priority "predicted to flood, but barely on record" areas.</li>
      <li>Use the satellite flood map as a discussion starting point in the hazard assessment, not as a replacement for it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="journalists" class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">For journalists</h2>
    <p class="text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      Key facts to verify before publishing:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2 mb-4">
      <li>In this version the prediction-vs-record gap compares what the model predicts (Track B) against what past records show (the Global Flood Database), NOT against the official hazard maps. The official-hazard-map comparison is a documented later step, held back because those government maps are locked behind logins or apps. A thin past record does not automatically mean an area is safe or that official data is wrong.</li>
      <li>Track A (the single-storm flood map) was checked against an independent 2015 flood map (Koppu); the overlap is low and openly reported (see methodology). Carina 2024 has no official measured record, so there is no official map to check the demo against.</li>
      <li>Population figures are population data (WorldPop) estimates summed up by province, not census counts. They may differ from the latest PSA estimates.</li>
      <li>The <a href="/methodology" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">methodology page</a> has the technical detail suitable for a methodology sidebar.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-xl mt-10 mb-2 text-ink-900">Sources and methodology references</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 text-base text-ink-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Sentinel-1 SAR flood mapping: <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/3/357" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">Twele et al., Remote Sensing 2016</a>; ESA Sentinel Application Platform.</li>
      <li>Global Flood Database (GFD): Tellman et al., Nature 2021. <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-021-03695-w" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">link</a></li>
      <li>MERIT Hydro: Yamazaki et al. 2019. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2019WR024873" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">link</a></li>
      <li>JRC Global Surface Water: Pekel et al., Nature 2016. <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/nature20584" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">link</a></li>
      <li>WorldPop Philippines: <a href="https://www.worldpop.org" class="text-accent-steel no-underline">worldpop.org</a>, CC-BY-4.0.</li>
      <li>AlphaEarth Foundations: Google / Google DeepMind, CC-BY-4.0.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
